/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  screen02_declared_rosinRammler

  A SCREEN ON A DECLARED CURVE.  The `screen` unit's second model,
  `declared`: no correlation predicts the cut, the case DECLARES the
  partition curve -- here the Rosin-Rammler form King (2001) eq. 4.41 prints
  for a vibrating screen,

      c(d) = 1 - exp[-0.693 (d/d50)^m]       (0.693 as printed),

  with d50 = 22 mm and m = 5.9 (King's value for a vibrating screen, p. 89),
  a bypass of 0.05 (fines misplaced to the oversize, every class) and the
  aperture (1 in), which is only checked against the grid.  Dry calcite.

  WHAT IT DEMONSTRATES: two branches no other case ran -- the `declared`
  screen and a Rosin-Rammler curve whose d50 and m the CASE supplies
  (screen01 reaches the same curve object only through Karra's d50 and
  King's fixed m).  Its curve's built-in check is the identity
  c(d50) = 1 - e^-0.693, true for ANY m; this case holds the whole curve:
  the oversize split, the cut sizes and the imperfection are recomputed by
  an independent closed-form script (verification/screenClosedForm.py,
  Python standard library, written from King's equations, not from the
  C++), and those values are the hand-written rows at the top of `expected`.

  WHAT ITS NUMBERS ARE NOT: a validation.  The feed and the curve's
  parameters are illustrative; nothing is measured.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
application   choupoSolve;
description   "A screen on a declared Rosin-Rammler partition curve (King 2001 eq. 4.41, d50 = 22 mm, m = 5.9, bypass 0.05) on an illustrative dry calcite feed: the whole curve held to an independent closed-form recomputation.";
verbosity     3;
citations
(
    "King, R.P. (2001) Modeling and Simulation of Mineral Processing Systems. Butterworth-Heinemann, Oxford. Section 4.2, eqs. 4.20-4.29 and 4.41 (p. 89)."
);
